1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,340
In 1719, two brothers had a Mughal emperor dragged from his palace,

2
00:00:05,560 --> 00:00:07,091
blinded and killed.

3
00:00:07,261 --> 00:00:10,931
No nobles had ever done that to an emperor.

4
00:00:11,101 --> 00:00:12,521
Within a single year,

5
00:00:12,731 --> 00:00:15,413
four different men on the Mughal throne.

6
00:00:15,583 --> 00:00:18,073
How did the greatest empire in the world

7
00:00:18,313 --> 00:00:20,578
become a prize to be handed out?

8
00:00:21,028 --> 00:00:25,068
Go back twelve years, to 1707.

9
00:00:25,238 --> 00:00:29,102
Emperor Aurangzeb ruled for nearly fifty years.

10
00:00:29,272 --> 00:00:32,452
He left three sons, one empire,

11
00:00:32,522 --> 00:00:33,862
and no agreed heir.

12
00:00:34,032 --> 00:00:37,993
So the princes did what princes had always done.

13
00:00:38,163 --> 00:00:39,191
They fought.

14
00:00:39,361 --> 00:00:43,981
A Mughal throne was never inherited. It had to be won.

15
00:00:44,151 --> 00:00:47,882
The oldest, Prince Muazzam, about 63.

16
00:00:48,052 --> 00:00:49,612
No young warrior,

17
00:00:49,752 --> 00:00:50,707
but he won.

18
00:00:50,877 --> 00:00:53,737
His brother Azam fell in battle that same year,

19
00:00:54,007 --> 00:00:57,840
and Kam Bakhsh died fighting him two years later.

20
00:00:58,010 --> 00:01:01,895
The old prince became Bahadur Shah the First.

21
00:01:02,065 --> 00:01:03,555
He won the war.

22
00:01:03,725 --> 00:01:06,394
Now he had to hold it together.

23
00:01:06,744 --> 00:01:10,045
His answer: make peace with everyone.

24
00:01:10,215 --> 00:01:13,875
Shahu, the Maratha prince held captive

25
00:01:14,135 --> 00:01:16,401
was set free and sent home.

26
00:01:16,571 --> 00:01:19,915
The Rajput kings confirmed in their lands.

27
00:01:20,085 --> 00:01:22,955
Only in the Punjab did he send his armies,

28
00:01:23,165 --> 00:01:25,840
against the Sikh leader, Banda Bahadur.

29
00:01:26,010 --> 00:01:31,019
Titles and rewards to keep every faction happy.

30
00:01:31,189 --> 00:01:34,399
Historian Khafi Khan gave him a nickname:

31
00:01:34,629 --> 00:01:36,007
Shah-i-Bekhabar.

32
00:01:36,177 --> 00:01:37,733
The unaware king.

33
00:01:37,903 --> 00:01:40,723
But the man who made peace with everyone

34
00:01:40,643 --> 00:01:42,831
left no plan for who came next.

35
00:01:43,381 --> 00:01:46,965
February 1712, Bahadur Shah died.

36
00:01:47,135 --> 00:01:50,153
His four sons went to war,

37
00:01:50,323 --> 00:01:52,846
The winner: Jahandar Shah.

38
00:01:53,016 --> 00:01:55,895
But the real power stood beside it.

39
00:01:56,065 --> 00:01:59,980
Chief minister Zulfikar Khan ran the empire.

40
00:02:00,150 --> 00:02:04,491
Jahandar held the crown. Zulfikar held the keys.

41
00:02:04,661 --> 00:02:06,631
He needed money fast,

42
00:02:06,841 --> 00:02:10,021
so: izara, revenue farming,

43
00:02:10,291 --> 00:02:14,515
a district's taxes went to whoever bid the most.

44
00:02:14,685 --> 00:02:18,632
Quick cash, but it squeezed the peasants.

45
00:02:18,802 --> 00:02:23,886
Jahandar abolished the jizya, the tax on non-Muslims.

46
00:02:24,056 --> 00:02:26,486
His reign lasted less than a year,

47
00:02:26,686 --> 00:02:30,107
because his nephew was raising an army.

48
00:02:30,407 --> 00:02:32,327
His name: Farrukhsiyar,

49
00:02:32,527 --> 00:02:34,522
and two powerful allies.

50
00:02:34,692 --> 00:02:36,204
The Sayyid brothers.

51
00:02:36,374 --> 00:02:40,838
An old family of soldiers, with their own armies.

52
00:02:41,008 --> 00:02:43,808
Abdullah Khan, also called Hassan Ali,

53
00:02:44,018 --> 00:02:46,453
and his younger brother, Hussain Ali.

54
00:02:46,623 --> 00:02:50,506
With their armies, he marched on Delhi.

55
00:02:50,676 --> 00:02:55,507
In 1713, Jahandar lost the throne, and his life.

56
00:02:55,677 --> 00:02:57,811
The brothers took the top jobs.

57
00:02:57,981 --> 00:03:01,807
One: chief minister. The other: the army.

58
00:03:01,977 --> 00:03:06,025
He wore the crown. They held the government.

59
00:03:06,195 --> 00:03:10,732
Jizya abolished again, and the pilgrimage tax.

60
00:03:10,902 --> 00:03:15,672
In 1717, royal orders: farmans,

61
00:03:15,942 --> 00:03:21,775
the English Company trades in Bengal duty free.

62
00:03:21,945 --> 00:03:28,243
A small favour. But one day, it would help take Bengal itself.

63
00:03:28,413 --> 00:03:31,363
He thought the brothers worked for him.

64
00:03:31,533 --> 00:03:33,864
He was about to learn the opposite.

65
00:03:34,414 --> 00:03:37,194
For six years, he plotted

66
00:03:37,414 --> 00:03:40,529
turning noble after noble against them.

67
00:03:40,699 --> 00:03:43,522
The brothers grew suspicious, and afraid.

68
00:03:43,692 --> 00:03:45,597
But the brothers struck first.

69
00:03:45,767 --> 00:03:48,237
Hussain Ali marched north from the Deccan,

70
00:03:48,497 --> 00:03:55,921
with him, Maratha troops sent by the Peshwa, Balaji Vishwanath.

71
00:03:56,091 --> 00:03:59,840
February 1719, they seized the palace.

72
00:04:00,010 --> 00:04:03,812
Farrukhsiyar dragged out, blinded, locked away.

73
00:04:03,982 --> 00:04:06,635
Within weeks, he was killed in prison.

74
00:04:06,805 --> 00:04:11,402
No Mughal emperor killed by his own nobles before.

75
00:04:11,572 --> 00:04:14,283
Now everyone knew the truth.

76
00:04:14,453 --> 00:04:17,572
The throne belonged to whoever held the army.

77
00:04:17,922 --> 00:04:21,181
The brothers chose the next emperor.

78
00:04:21,351 --> 00:04:24,556
First: Rafi-ud-Darajat.

79
00:04:24,726 --> 00:04:27,394
Just over three months.

80
00:04:27,564 --> 00:04:32,278
His brother crowned as Shah Jahan II.

81
00:04:32,448 --> 00:04:35,014
About three months too.

82
00:04:35,184 --> 00:04:37,864
A third puppet.

83
00:04:38,034 --> 00:04:41,940
A 17-year-old prince, Raushan Akhtar.

84
00:04:42,110 --> 00:04:46,727
September 1719: crowned Muhammad Shah.

85
00:04:46,897 --> 00:04:49,447
Four men on the throne in one year,

86
00:04:49,677 --> 00:04:52,864
every one owed it to the same two brothers.

87
00:04:53,034 --> 00:04:56,872
A teenager would be easy to control.

88
00:04:57,042 --> 00:04:58,165
They were wrong.

89
00:04:58,715 --> 00:05:02,207
Muhammad Shah waited, and found allies.

90
00:05:02,377 --> 00:05:04,857
The most important: Nizam-ul-Mulk,

91
00:05:05,107 --> 00:05:08,497
a noble with his own army in the Deccan.

92
00:05:08,667 --> 00:05:11,383
Together, they moved against the brothers.

93
00:05:11,553 --> 00:05:15,918
In 1720, Hussain Ali was murdered.

94
00:05:16,088 --> 00:05:20,853
Abdullah beaten in battle, taken prisoner.

95
00:05:21,023 --> 00:05:24,796
Died in 1722, poisoned.

96
00:05:24,966 --> 00:05:27,003
The kingmakers were gone.

97
00:05:27,173 --> 00:05:29,111
But the damage was done.

98
00:05:29,561 --> 00:05:32,201
Once nobles could make and break emperors,

99
00:05:32,451 --> 00:05:35,509
the emperor stopped being the centre.

100
00:05:35,679 --> 00:05:40,277
Every governor, every general, building their own power.

101
00:05:40,447 --> 00:05:46,710
Deccan, Awadh, Bengal: ruling like kings.

102
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:49,997
Historian Sidney Owen

103
00:05:50,167 --> 00:05:56,362
Bahadur Shah I "was the last Emperor of whom anything favourable can be said."

104
00:05:56,531 --> 00:06:00,270
A harsh verdict on every emperor who came after.

105
00:06:00,720 --> 00:06:03,727
Not in one battle.

106
00:06:03,897 --> 00:06:09,292
It fell when the throne became a prize to give away.

107
00:06:09,842 --> 00:06:13,132
Muhammad Shah would rule 29 years.

108
00:06:13,302 --> 00:06:15,132
But in 1739,

109
00:06:15,332 --> 00:06:18,255
a king from Persia was coming for his throne.
